---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { SITE } from "../lib/site.mjs";
import { COVERAGE_CAVEAT } from "../../../scripts/lib/data.mjs";

const statuses = [
  ["Announced", "The person or media report an intention to stand. Party endorsement is not confirmed.", "A public statement by the person or party, or a named news report."],
  ["Endorsed", "The party has preselected or endorsed the person for that seat.", "A party announcement, or a news report citing the party."],
  ["Formally nominated", "A nomination has been accepted by the electoral commission.", "The commission's nominations list."],
  ["Withdrawn", "The candidacy ended, before or after nomination.", "A statement or news report. Any published reason is recorded neutrally."],
  ["Disendorsed", "The party revoked its endorsement.", "A party statement or news report. Wording stays with the sourced fact."],
  ["Elected", "Declared elected.", "The commission's declaration."],
  ["Defeated", "Contested and not elected.", "The commission's results."],
];
---

<Base
  title="Methodology"
  description="How the Australian Election Tracker defines candidate statuses, what evidence each requires, how coverage is counted, and how corrections are handled."
>
  <div class="wrap wrap-narrow section">
    <p class="eyebrow">How this works</p>
    <h1>Methodology</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      One set of published rules, applied identically to every party and every independent.
      These rules are enforced mechanically: a record without a source fails our automated
      checks and cannot be published.
    </p>

    <h2>Candidate statuses</h2>
    <p>
      A candidacy holds exactly one current status. Every previous status stays in its
      history, with the date and the source that supported it. Rumour and speculation are
      not statuses and are not recorded at all.
    </p>

    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="data">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Status</th>
            <th scope="col">Meaning</th>
            <th scope="col">Minimum evidence</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            statuses.map(([name, meaning, evidence]) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{name}</th>
                <td>{meaning}</td>
                <td class="muted">{evidence}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 id="sourcing">Sourcing rules</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Every status entry requires at least one source, recorded as a structured record:
        URL, publisher, title, date published and date accessed.
      </li>
      <li>
        Primary sources (electoral commission, party, candidate) are preferred over
        secondary ones. Secondary sources are named by outlet — never "reports say".
      </li>
      <li>
        Paywalled sources are acceptable if they are publicly citable. An archive link is
        added where possible.
      </li>
      <li>
        Social media counts only from the verified or official account of the party or
        candidate.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="coverage">How coverage is counted</h2>
    <p>
      A party "covers" a district when we hold at least one individually sourced record of
      a live candidacy — announced, endorsed, formally nominated or elected — for that
      party in that district.
    </p>
    <div class="notice" role="note">
      <strong>The limitation you should know about.</strong> {COVERAGE_CAVEAT}
    </div>
    <p>
      This matters most for sitting members. It is reasonable to assume most incumbents
      will recontest, but an assumption is not a source, so we do not record one. A party
      that holds many seats will therefore appear to have lower coverage than it really
      does until each of those seats is individually verified. We would rather publish a
      number that understates reality than one we cannot evidence.
    </p>
    <p>
      The inverse also holds: a district with no recorded candidate means we have not
      verified one, not that nobody is standing. Nominations for the 2026 Victorian state
      election do not close until 13 November 2026, and the Victorian Electoral Commission
      is the authoritative source for the final ballot.
    </p>

    <h2 id="corrections">Corrections</h2>
    <p>
      Errors are corrected by a new commit. History is never rewritten — the original
      record, the correction, and the reason all remain visible in the public Git history.
    </p>
    <p>
      Anyone may request a correction. Requests concerning living people are prioritised.
      Include a public source if you have one.
    </p>
    <p><a class="btn" href={SITE.issues}>Submit a correction</a></p>

    <h2 id="neutrality">Neutrality</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Every registered party and independent receives identical data structure, rules and
        display treatment.
      </li>
      <li id="ordering">
        <strong>Ordering.</strong> Party listings are ordered by the number of seats the
        party currently holds in the Parliament of Victoria (both houses), largest first,
        then alphabetically. That is an objective, externally verifiable fact about the
        sitting parliament. Listings are never ordered by candidate coverage, campaign
        activity, polling or any editorial judgement of importance — and district listings
        are ordered by seat name.
      </li>
      <li>
        Party colours appear only inside party-specific data visualisation. The platform's
        own identity is deliberately not any party's colour.
      </li>
      <li>
        No payment, sponsorship or advertising can affect coverage, ordering or
        verification. Any sponsorship would be disclosed on this site.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="privacy">What is never published</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Unverified submissions.</li>
      <li>The identity or contact details of anyone who submits information.</li>
      <li>
        Personal information about candidates beyond their public candidacy — no home
        addresses, family details or employers, unless the candidate has published them
        themselves in that context.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="polls">Public polls and the tracker average</h2>
    <p>
      In addition to the candidate ledger, the project collects publicly released
      statewide Victorian Legislative Assembly primary-vote polls as sourced records and
      publishes a transparent weighted average with uncertainty bands.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Inclusion rules, time window, weights and error bars are fixed in
        <a href="https://github.com/coldix/elections/blob/main/docs/POLL-METHODOLOGY.md">
          docs/POLL-METHODOLOGY.md</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Party, union and advocacy-commissioned polls may be recorded for transparency and
        are excluded from the average by default (rare documented exceptions only).
      </li>
      <li>
        The average is a summary of recent public polling — not a forecast, seat model or
        preference recommendation.
      </li>
      <li>
        Poll figures never change party or district ordering on this site (see
        <a href="#ordering">ordering</a>).
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Live figures: <a href="/polls">poll average</a>. Exports:
      <a href="/data/vic2026/polls.json">polls.json</a>,
      <a href="/data/vic2026/poll-average.json">poll-average.json</a>.
    </p>

    <p class="foot muted">
      Candidate rules on this page mirror <code>docs/METHODOLOGY.md</code> in the
      <a href={SITE.repo}>public repository</a>. Poll rules live in
      <code>docs/POLL-METHODOLOGY.md</code>. Both carry their own change history.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  h2 {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
  }
  ul {
    padding-left: 1.15rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
  .notice {
    margin: 1.25rem 0;
  }
  .foot {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    padding-top: 1.25rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
</style>
